      "content": "Task Guide: Task: Task: Identify Adsorption Sites\nDescription: Determine possible adsorption sites on the chosen slab and save the results as a JSON file. Submit the path to the JSON file.\n\nRequired submission format:\npath to json file (eg. adsorption_sites.json)\n\n\nAvailable input data:\n- Input from cu20_choose_slab: /Users/n0w0f/git/n0w0f_2026/corral_fixes/corral/workdir/4/cu20_enumerate_slabs_trial_8/slabs.json\n\nIMPORTANT: You have access to filesystem tools. All files will be saved in your isolated workspace.\n\n\nThis task uses output from tasks: cu20_choose_slab (available)\n\nAvailable Tools:\nTool: list_files\nDescription: List files in a directory\nArguments:\n- path (str, required): Path to the directory\n- recursive (bool, optional, default: False): Whether to list files recursively\n\nTool: read_file\nDescription: Read the contents of a file into a string\nArguments:\n- path (str, required): Path to the file to read\n\nTool: write_file\nDescription: Write content to a file\nArguments:\n- path (str, required): Path to the file to write\n- content (str, required): Content to write to the file\n\nTool: file_info\nDescription: Get information about a file or directory\nArguments:\n- path (str, required): Path to the file or directory\n\nTool: cat_files\nDescription: Concatenate and display contents of one or more files\nArguments:\n- paths (list[str], required): List of file paths to concatenate\n- separator (str, optional, default: \n): Separator between file contents\n\nTool: copy_file\nDescription: Copy a file from a source to destination\nArguments:\n- source (str, required): Source file path\n- destination (str, required): Destination file path\n\nTool: get_adsorption_sites_text\nDescription: Identify and classify all possible adsorption sites on a surface slab.\n\nThis tool analyzes a surface slab structure to identify and classify potential adsorption sites where molecules can bind.\n    It uses geometric and chemical analysis to determine different types of binding sites such as top sites (above surface atoms), bridge sites (between two atoms), and hollow sites (in multi-atom depressions).\n    This analysis is fundamental for understanding surface reactivity and designing catalysts.\n\nWhen to use: When to use this tool:\n    - Use when you need to identify all possible adsorption sites on a surface\n    - Best suited for systematic studies of surface reactivity and catalysis\n    - Essential for understanding how molecules interact with surfaces\n    - Recommended before placing adsorbates to understand binding options\n    - Avoid when you already know the specific binding site coordinates\n\nHow this tool works:\n    - Parses the slab CIF structure to identify surface atoms\n    - Uses AdsorbateSiteFinder from pymatgen to geometrically analyze the surface topology\n    - Classifies sites based on coordination environment (ontop, bridge, hollow)\n    - Calculates fractional coordinates for each potential binding site\n    - Returns sites organized by type in a JSON format for easy selection\n\nWorkflow: Typical workflow integration example:\n    1.  First obtain a slab structure using choose_slab_text or create_slab_from_structure_text \n    2.  Apply this tool to identify all adsorption sites on the surface \n    3.  Use choose_adsorption_site_text to select a specific site for adsorbate placement\nArguments:\n- slab_cif (str, required): CIF string of the surface slab structure.\nA properly formatted CIF string containing the surface slab structure with atomic positions, lattice parameters, and surface geometry. This should be a two-dimensional periodic structure with a well-defined surface and vacuum region. The structure is analyzed to identify potential adsorption sites.\n\nHow to use tools:\n1. Each tool call must specify the tool name and required arguments\n2. Tools may return errors if arguments are invalid\n3. You can make multiple tool calls as needed\n4. All tool calls are recorded and affect your final score\n\nExample tool call format:\n{\n    \"tool_name\": \"tool_name\",\n    \"arguments\": {\n        \"arg1\": value1,\n        \"arg2\": value2\n    }\n}\n\n\nNone\n\nThink about what to do next and respond in the following format:\n\n<thought>[your reasoning]</thought>\n<action>[tool name]</action>\n<action_input>[tool arguments as JSON]</action_input>. For tool calls without arguments, use `<action_input>{}</action_input>`\n\nIf you have the final answer, respond with:\n<thought>[your reasoning]</thought>\n<final_answer>[answer]</final_answer>\n\n\n\nThe code expects the XML tags to be used exactly as shown. If this format is not followed, the regex parsing will not work."
